<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">24 May in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 24 May, from 1543 to 1941.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1543 CE <span class="tih-ago">483 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Nicolaus Copernicus dies in Frombork</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Copernicus died weeks after his On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres was printed; tradition says he saw a copy on his deathbed. His Sun-centred model began a revolution in astronomy.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/england" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/england.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1689 CE <span class="tih-ago">337 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Toleration Act grants freedom of worship to Dissenters</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Act allowed Protestant Nonconformists in England to worship in their own licensed meeting houses. Catholics and anti-Trinitarians were excluded.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1738 CE <span class="tih-ago">288 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">John Wesley feels his heart 'strangely warmed'</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At a meeting in Aldersgate Street, London, John Wesley experienced an assurance of faith while hearing Luther's preface to Romans read. The experience energised the Methodist movement.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1844 CE <span class="tih-ago">182 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Samuel Morse sends 'What hath God wrought'</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Morse tapped out the biblical phrase from the Capitol in Washington to Alfred Vail in Baltimore. The line opened the age of the electric telegraph in the United States.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1883 CE <span class="tih-ago">143 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Brooklyn Bridge opens across the East River</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After fourteen years of construction, the bridge linking Manhattan and Brooklyn opened. It was then the longest suspension bridge in the world, the first to use steel wire cables.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1941 CE <span class="tih-ago">85 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Bismarck sinks HMS Hood in the Denmark Strait</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A shell from the German battleship detonated Hood's magazine, and she sank in minutes; only three of her 1,418 crew survived. The Royal Navy hunted Bismarck down three days later.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a></div></div></li></ol>
